Under non-differential misclassification with $C_{kk} > 0$ for all $k$ (no category has zero self-classification probability), and provided the true effect and the omitted-variable bias share the same sign (a condition satisfied under standard DGP parameterizations), the following topologies satisfy $\tau_{\text{sign\_flip}} = 0$: confounding, mediation, collider, M-bias, and front-door. Sign reversal is possible only in the exposure and IV topologies.
\end{proposition}

\emph{Proof sketch.}
For confounding and mediation, a Frisch--Waugh--Lovell decomposition shows the bias is $B(C) = R(C)/D(C)$, where $D(C)>0$ and $|R(C)|$ is bounded by the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality. The bound ensures $|B(C)| < |\tau|$ whenever the true effect and the omitted-variable (or total) effect share the same sign, guaranteeing sign preservation for all column-stochastic $C$. This sign-alignment condition is parameter-dependent but holds generically: it is satisfied whenever the confounder's effect on the treatment and outcome have the same direction, which is the standard assumption motivating adjustment in the first place.

